      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>The John N. Calley Foundation is a California private foundation that supports educational opportunities for talented young people whose abilities may go unrecognised. It honours the legacy of film executive and producer John Calley, whose career was marked by backing creative talent. Its grantmaking supports youth-serving organisations and emerging leaders.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "The Calley Foundation works with organisations that help young people develop skills and pursue opportunities in the arts, education and community life. Its approach recognises forms of promise that may not be captured by grades or conventional academic awards.\n\nThrough collaboration with LA2050 and the Goldhirsh Foundation, it has supported Los Angeles programmes spanning music instruction, mathematics, youth housing and leadership development. Its board sets the foundation's mission, strategy and budget and reviews the progress of its funding. Recipients have included the Los Angeles Maritime Institute and the Fernando Pullum Community Arts Center.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Official mission, listed recipient organisations and governance description support the profile; no grant outcomes or transient totals are asserted."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 93,
    "html": "<p>The Calley Foundation works with organisations that help young people develop skills and pursue opportunities in the arts, education and community life. Its approach recognises forms of promise that may not be captured by grades or conventional academic awards.</p>\r\n<p>Through collaboration with LA2050 and the Goldhirsh Foundation, it has supported Los Angeles programmes spanning music instruction, mathematics, youth housing and leadership development. Its board sets the foundation's mission, strategy and budget and reviews the progress of its funding. Recipients have included the Los Angeles Maritime Institute and the Fernando Pullum Community Arts Center.</p>\r\n"
  },
